  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The State of Indiana received $3.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.3B, making defense work the dominant source of federal spending in the state. Federal award activity included 15,486 awards with an average award size of $212K, and obligations fell 53.1% year over year. Contracting activity has varied across recent fiscal years: FY21 was $6.3B, FY22 was $8.2B, FY23 was $6B, FY24 was $5.3B, FY25 was $7B, and FY26 is $3.3B so far. The largest vendor by obligations is ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION with $784M in awards, followed by UNDERSEA SENSOR SYSTEMS, INC. with $243M and LMI CONSULTING, LLC with $200M. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $721M, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $333M and Engineering Services at $285M. The current-year totals are partial because fiscal year to date figures are still accumulating.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers in Indiana?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the biggest buyer with $2.3B in obligations. The Department of Homeland Security follows with $258M, and the Department of Health and Human Services follows with $195M. Several civil agencies also contribute, including the Department of Veterans Affairs at $170M and the General Services Administration at $75.8M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries capture the most federal contract dollars in Indiana?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing leads with $721M in obligations. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing is next with $333M, followed by Engineering Services at $285M. Other notable industries include Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $207M and Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $172M."
      }
    ]
